<script>
import { url } from '@abide/abide'
import Doc from '#src/lib/Doc.abide'
import Code from '#src/lib/Code.abide'
import Example from '#src/lib/Example.abide'
import Section from '#src/lib/Section.abide'
import AccountInvoice from './lib/AccountInvoice.abide'
import Phone from './lib/Phone.abide'
import Statement from './lib/Statement.abide'
import accountInvoiceSource from './lib/AccountInvoice.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import phoneSource from './lib/Phone.abide' with { type: 'text' }
import statementSource from './lib/Statement.abide' with { type: 'text' }
</script>

<Doc>
    <Section title="The two forms of a refusal">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>refuse.named(name, status?, message?, options?)</code></td><td>a factory for a refusal a caller can name and narrow</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>refuse(status, message?)</code></td><td>an undeclared refusal at a status</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>refuse.named</code> hands back a factory, and the factory carries the type, so
        there is no registry to consult and nothing to keep in step. Its status defaults to
        <strong>400</strong> rather than 500, a declared refusal being by construction an expected
        answer, and its message to the status's phrase. Data is declared by
        <code>options.schema</code> and by nothing else: the schema types it, so the name is written
        once, and checks it synchronously at construction, where a <code>message</code> given as a
        function runs too, on the data. Declaring in a module both sides import lets a page and a
        handler use the same names.</p>

        <p><code>refuse(status)</code> is for where the status <em>is</em> the answer. It carries no
        data and has no options bag to put any in, data undeclared having no type on the other side.
        You return it like any other refusal (17.4), so <code>return refuse(404)</code> stands as a
        guard. The message defaults to the status registry's phrase, which makes
        <code>refuse(404)</code> a whole refusal on its own. Every signature is in the
        <a href={url('/reference/refusals')}>Refusals</a> reference.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A refusal is narrowed by name, where the value is read">
        <Example
            id="refuse-narrowed"
            title="`rpc.isError(e, name)` — the refusal, matched by name"
            summary="Three invoices on one account. Open the second and the third — each is refused for a different reason."
            files={{
                'lib/AccountInvoice.abide': accountInvoiceSource,
            }}
        >
            <AccountInvoice />
        </Example>

        <p><em>2 refusals, 0 names to keep in step</em> — the arm matches the same two strings, and
        nothing tells it when one of them changes.</p>

        <p>The refusal lands in the <code>Failures</code> of the <code>Reactive</code> that produced
        it, so it is read where the value is read rather than caught somewhere else. On a handler
        that read is <code>rpc.isError</code>, <strong>narrowed by what that handler
        declared</strong>: matching the name gives back <code>.data</code> with the schema's type on
        it, plus <code>.status</code> and <code>.name</code>. Reaching for a sibling value's
        <code>isError</code> is a compile error where the unions differ.</p>

        <p>A handler refuses by <strong>returning</strong>, and that is the one spelling.
        Construction is inert — <code>notYours(data)</code> builds a <code>Failed</code> and does
        not throw — which puts the refusal in the handler's return type honestly, rather than by
        <code>never</code> vanishing from a union. <code>Rpc</code>'s third type parameter picks it
        up from there. <code>throw notYours(data)</code> refuses identically and loses only the
        caller's ability to name it.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-isnt-there-yet')}>Loading states</a> ·
        <a href={url('/templates/show-markup-conditionally')}>Conditionals</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A refusal the page made itself is read the same way">
        <Example
            id="refuse-write"
            title="`s.isError(e, name)` — a refusal the page made itself"
            summary="A contact's phone, normalized on the way in. Take a digit out and press Save."
            files={{
                'lib/Phone.abide': phoneSource,
            }}
        >
            <Phone />
        </Example>

        <p>A refusal resolves on both sides, so a <code>transform</code> in a page refuses a write
        exactly as a handler refuses a request, and it fills the same <code>Failures</code>.
        <code>s.isError</code> is <code>Reactive</code>'s own, on every value;
        <code>rpc.isError</code> is that member narrowed by one handler's declarations.</p>

        <p>A refused write stores nothing, so the record keeps the value it had and the reason sits
        beside it rather than in place of it. The next write that is accepted clears the standing
        failure.</p>

        <p>What you refuse with decides which gate you reach for. A <code>schema</code> refuses as a
        <code>ValidationError</code> carrying the messages; a <code>transform</code> refuses as a
        name you declared, which is the only way a refusal arrives under its own name with its own
        data.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/values/show-a-value-that-changes')}>Local state</a> ·
        <a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `Failed` built and discarded is a compile error">
        <p>Because construction is inert, the line below would fall through and pay the invoice
        anyway:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="server" code={`if (!invoice.mine)
    notYours({ owner: invoice.owner })`} />

        <p>So an expression statement whose type is <code>Failed</code> is refused at compile time,
        naming the two repairs: <code>return</code> it, or <code>throw</code> it. Detectable in
        syntax, which is why the guard form does not have to be given up to make construction
        inert.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`Failed<Name, Data>` is the one refusal type">
        <p>In-process and over a wire alike. There is no second, http-shaped one:</p>

        <Code language="ts" caption="shared" code={`type Failed<Name, Data> = Error & {
    name: Name
    status: number
    message: string
    data: Data
}`} />

        <p>Structural, because a caller catches a shape rather than a class it imported. Over the
        wire a refusal serializes as <code>{'{ name, message, data }'}</code> and hydrates back into
        that shape, the status coming from the response.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="abide's own names: three you can narrow, and one you cannot">
        <p>Both built with <code>refuse.named</code> like any other:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th></th><th>Status</th><th>Returned where</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>notFound</code></td><td>404</td><td>no route matched, or no handler is mounted at that address and method</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>validationError</code></td><td>422</td><td>a schema refused the arguments</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>aborted</code></td><td>499</td><td>every call waiting on a load handed a <code>signal</code>, and every one of them aborted</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>notFound</code> carries <code>{'{ path, method }'}</code>, so an error page can say
        which address. <code>validationError</code> carries <code>Issues&lt;Args&gt;</code>, keyed
        by path, and <code>rpc.isError(e, 'ValidationError')</code> narrows <code>e.data</code> to
        it with nothing declared. <code>aborted</code> carries nothing, and only reaches a value
        whose callers asked for it.</p>

        <p><code>HttpError</code> is the third name and the last. It is what
        <code>refuse(status)</code> hands back: undeclared, entering no <code>Failures</code> union,
        and nothing can narrow to it. It is still a name a caller reads, in a wire body, an OpenAPI
        default response and an MCP error entry, and that name is http-shaped because those are the
        only places it surfaces. In a browser <code>refuse</code> throws the way
        <code>config()</code> does — a status is a response's business. The rest of what abide
        raises — 403 on an origin mismatch, 413 over-size, 504 on a timeout — has no data to narrow
        <strong>to</strong>, so it stays <code>HttpError</code> at its status.</p>

        <p>Three and no more. A name is public surface forever.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`aborted` is what a canceled load reads">
        <Example
            id="aborted-signal"
            title="`aborted` — what a load reads once every caller stopped waiting"
            summary="A contact's year statement from a slow ledger. Load it and cancel before it lands."
            files={{
                'lib/Statement.abide': statementSource,
            }}
        >
            <Statement />
        </Example>

        <p>A call handed a <code>signal</code> is one caller saying it can stop waiting. Where every
        call that reached the load passed one and every one of them aborted, the load ends (16.48):
        <code>busy()</code> goes false, the request to the server is aborted, and the value refuses
        with <code>aborted</code> at 499 rather than throwing (16.49). <code>aborted.is</code>
        narrows it like any other factory's refusal, so the page tells "you canceled this" apart
        from "this failed".</p>

        <p>The next call starts a new load under its own signal (16.62), which is why Load works
        again after a cancel. See <a href={url('/data/put-a-ceiling-on-a-request')}>Limits</a> for
        the signal itself.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A bare failure narrows through the factory's own `is`">
        <p><code>error.abide</code> receives a bare failure as a prop, so there is no value to hang
        <code>s.isError</code> off. Every factory carries its own <code>is</code> for exactly
        that:</p>

        <Code language="abide" caption="src/error.abide — excerpt" code={`{#if notFound.is(failure)}
    <h1>No such page</h1>
    <p>Nothing answers {failure.data.method} {failure.data.path}.</p>
{:else if notYours.is(failure)}
    <h1>Not yours</h1>
{:else}
    <h1>Something went wrong</h1>
    <p>{failure.message}</p>
{/if}`} />

        <p>The factory carries the type, so this needs no registry either.</p>

        <p>Read on: <a href={url('/pages/show-a-page-when-something-fails')}>Error pages</a></p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Next">
        <ul>
            <li><a href={url('/data/check-what-callers-send-you')}>Schemas</a> — where
            <code>validationError</code> comes from</li>
            <li><a href={url('/pages/show-a-page-when-something-fails')}>Error pages</a> — the page
            a refusal renders in</li>
            <li><a href={url('/data/decide-who-may-call-what')}>Authorization</a> — refusing
            before the handler runs</li>
        </ul>
    </Section>
</Doc>
